1. Regard each piece as a polyomino made of unit squares. Allowing rotations but not reflections gives:
   - \(A\): \(L\)-triomino;
   - \(B\): straight tetromino;
   - \(C\): skew tetromino;
   - \(D\): \(L\)-pentomino;
   - \(E\): stair-shaped pentomino.

2. Test every legal rotation of each piece in which it covers the dotted square. After each trial, continue filling the first uncovered square with the remaining pieces.

3. The completion counts are:
\[
\begin{array}{c|ccccc}
\text{Piece on the dot} & A & B & C & D & E\\ \hline
\text{Valid complete tilings} & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 1
\end{array}
\]

4. Only one rotation of \(E\) allows the remaining pieces \(A,B,C,D\) to fit without gaps or overlaps. Thus the dotted square is covered by piece \(E\).

\[
\boxed{E}
\]
